#***************************************************************************************************
# This site is hosted on Cloudflare Workers which doesn't use .htaccess (it uses "rules" in the
# dashboard). Below, I show my:
#
#		- .htaccess from Apache hosting (not used)
#   	- Cloudflare rules (used).
#***************************************************************************************************

#===============================================================================
# When this site was hosted on Apache, the following was used:
#RewriteEngine On
#
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Redirect apex (non-www domain name) to www:
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^mcacc-collars\.charity$ [NC]
#RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/$1 [R=301,L]
#
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Require https:
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#RewriteCond %{HTTPS} off
#RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/$1 [R=301,L]
#
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Redirect a free domain to my domain name:
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^.*\.webs\.nf$ [NC]
#RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/$1 [R=301,L]


#===================================================================================================
# ON CLOUDFLARE:
#
# REDIRECT HTTP & APEX TO WWW: 
#     The http & non-www redirect can be done in two places. 
#
# 1. I do it in "bulk redirects:"
#   - Go to home (domains) > "Delivery & performance > Bulk redirects."
# 	- Create a "bulk redirect list" with these three items:
#	 
#   SOURCE URL                        TARGET URL                         STATUS
#   http://mcacc-collars.charity/     https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/ 301
#   https://mcacc-collars.charity/    "                                  301
#   http://www.mcacc-collars.charity/ "                                  301
#
#   You MUST enable the following "edit parameters" for EACH rule:
#     - Subpath matching (matches any source URL after /) 
#     - Preserve path suffix (adds anything after / to the target URL) 
#
# IMPORTANT: You MUST disable "SSL/TLS > Edge certificates > Always Use HTTPS". That looks tempting
# to use, but there is a condition where two redirects will occur (someone visits http://apex/ will
# be redircted to https, and then the bulk redirect will redirect https://apex/ to
# https://www.apex/). It's better to cover both conditions in one redirect.
#
# Redirect rules are processed late in Cloudflare's process.[1] I use it because I block a lot of spam
# traffic. The blocking occurs before the bulk redirect (therefore less to redirect).
#
# The bulk-redirect matching algorithm[2] suggests that it can match just hostname (without scheme),
# but I couldn't get that to work. (If that worked, only one list item would be necessary. I created
# two to cover the two possible ways non-www can occur.
#
#
# 2. You can also do it in "Rules > Overview > Redirect rules" with the following rule:
#
#   "If incoming requests match..."
#     - "Custom filter expression"
#       - "When incoming requests match..."
#         - Chose "Edit expression:"
#           (not ssl) or (http.host eq "mcacc-collars.charity")
#	  
#       - "Then..."
#         - "URL redirect"
#           Type: Dynamic
#           Expression: concat("https://www.mcacc-collars.charity", http.request.uri.path)
#           Status: 301
#
# Rules > Redirect rules is applied very early in Cloudflare's processing. That would be better for
# your normal traffic, but I block a lot of spam traffic. Using "redirect rules" redirects those
# only to be blocked when the return (with https & www.). Seems wasteful. Plus, you see the first
# (redirected) spam listed in "Security > analytics." That can add a lot of noise there. If there
# were a way to not see it there (not log those redirects), I'd probably use this rule. I chose
# "bulk redirects" because the "Security > analytics" is more meaningful (blocking the spam before
# the redirect).
#
# REDIRECT /images/[subdirectory] to /images
# I also created another bulk-redirect list for the /images/medium/ and /small/ subdirectories. They
# have smaller versions of the images in /images. I don't have directory browsing for those subdirs.
# I redirect any request (to browse those subdirs) to /images, which has directory browsing.
#
#
# SOURCE URL                     								TARGET URL									STATUS
# https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/images/medium				https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/images/ 	301
# https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/images/medium/				"											301
# https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/images/medium/index.html	"											301
# https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/images/small				"											301
# https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/images/small/				"											301
# https://www.mcacc-collars.charity/images/small/index.html		"											301
#
# Do not enable any "Edit parameters."
#
# I also bulk=-edirect 3 other things as a convenience. "/license.txt" to "/LICENSE.txt",
# "/manifest.webmanifest" to "site.webmanifest." Also, google occasionally requests its site-verify
# file (xxx.html) without the .html. I redirect that.
#
# See /_redirects for more redirects. I use that file when I change a file or path name. Eventually,
# I remove those redirects when nobody's trying to access the old name.
#
#
#===
# BLOCK MALICIOUS REQUESTS
#
# I was getting hundreds of requests for system files, things I don't have (nobody should try to
# access even if I did). I created the following rules in Security > Security rules > Custom rules:" 
#
# RULE #1: Whitelist: expected (valid) requests
#
# The blocking rule (below) have so many conditions that it seemed better to whitelist what I
# know to be the valid requests. I start with what should be the most frequent requests (images?),
# end with the least expected (people typing in a path without a trailing /).
#
# I think /images/* get the most access. I expect four extensions (.webp, .jpg, .gif or .svg; plus
# .html whitelisted elsewhere). The shortest image filename is 21-char (plus 8 for "/images/" path):

(starts_with(http.request.uri.path, "/images/") and
  substring(http.request.uri.path, -4) in {"webp" ".jpg" ".gif" ".svg"} and
  len(http.request.uri.path) gt 28
) or

# There can also be directory browsing on /images/ (not the subdirs inside it). But, that should be
# much less frequent. I whitelist it below. The .html extension is handled there.
#
# Next, I assume /css & /js are the next most-common requests:

http.request.uri.path in {"/css/style.css" "/css/files.css" "/js/darkmode.js"} or

# Again, there could be directory browsing on those. That's whitelisted below because it should be
# rare.
#
# I think the published (displayable) pages (directories) of the site would be next most commonly
# requested (in the order I think they'd be most visited, not sure if order matters with "in"?):

http.request.uri.path in {
"/resources/"
"/resources/leash-sleeve/"
"/resources/collars-leashes/"
"/resources/collars-leashes/lk1900bn/"
"/resources/collars-leashes/lk1900bn/bobbin-winder/"
"/resources/collars-leashes/hot-knife/"
"/"} or

# Those can also be directory browsed with /index.html (or without the trailing /). This should be
# even more rare. I whitelist it below.
#
# Then there's requests for /files/*. Only the filetypes pdf & zip exist: 

( starts_with(http.request.uri.path, "/files/") and
  substring(http.request.uri.path, -3) in {"pdf" "zip"}
) or

# Again, /files could be directory browsed with /index.html or without trailing /. Whitelisted below.
#
# Next, I enumerate all the individual files acessible in home:

http.request.uri.path in {"/BingSiteAuth.xml" "/googleb3737839f3b1fec8.html" "/llms.txt" "/robots.txt" "/site.webmanifest" "/404.html" "/404.svg" "/sitemap.xml.gz" "/sitemap.xml" "/LICENSE.txt" "/ads.txt" "/6s9dh98hxq1sjut3sf1t1pq5rz41uevv.txt"
"/android-chrome-192x192.png" "/android-chrome-512x512.png" "/apple-touch-icon.png" "/favicon.ico" "/favicon-16x16.png" "/favicon-32x32.png" "/icon.svg"

# I dont serve these files, but redirect them to files I do serve:
"/license.txt" "/manifest.webmanifest" "/googleb3737839f3b1fec8"

# I don't serve these, but see requests for them. I wouldn't block these (they're not bad). So, 
# I whitelist them.
"/humans.txt /sitemap_index.xml"
} or

# There's also .well-known. I think that's supposed to be wide-open, any request allowed (a
# standard). But, .php requests look unreasonable to me. (I'd be careful blocking more.).

(starts_with(http.request.uri.path, "/.well-known/") and
  not ends_with(http.request.uri.path, ".php")
) or

# Directory browsing shouldn't be allowed for /.well-known. I don't whitelist .index.html nor
# requests without the trailing / .

# At this point, the only other requests that could occur would be directory browsing of either 1)
# directories that aren't the site's "pages" people are invited to visit (whitelisted above as
# directories). Or, 2) directories that were whitelisted above, but could be requested with
# "index.html" added (which would be rare).
#
# The following are in the order of what's already been whitelisted (what this adds to, but done
# here because these requests should be very rare.

http.request.uri.path in {

# /directory/ & /directory/index.html

"/images/" "/images/index.html"
"/css/" "/css/index.html"
"/js/" "/js/index.html"

# These are just /directory/index.html because the /directory/ was already whitelisted above:

"/resources/index.html"
"/resources/collars-leashes/index.html"
"/resources/collars-leashes/hot-knife/index.html"
"/resources/collars-leashes/lk1900bn/index.html"
"/resources/collars-leashes/lk1900bn/bobbin-winder/index.html"
"/resources/leash-sleeve/index.html"
"/index.html"

# Another /directory/ & /directory/index.html

"/files/" "/files/index.html"

# It's also possible to browse the subdirs of /images/, but there's nothing there to see. I use
# bulk-redirects to redirect these accesses to /images/

"/images/small/" "/images/small/index.html"
"/images/medium/" "/images/medium/index.html"} or

# Finally, the rarest request would be any directory browsing without the trailing slash. I think
# this could only occur if someone typed the url, very rare.

http.request.uri.path in {
"/images"
"/css"
"/js"
"/resources"
"/resources/collars-leashes"
"/resources/collars-leashes/hot-knife"
"/resources/collars-leashes/lk1900bn"
"/resources/collars-leashes/lk1900bn/bobbin-winder"
"/resources/leash-sleeve"
"/files"
"/images/small"
"/images/medium"}

# Action: SKIP (all remaining custom rules)
# Log matching requests: NO
#
# Note: It's tempting to consider reversing the logic, and say "if NOT" any of the above: block
# (everthing else). But, I think there could be legitimate things browsers, AI bots might look for.
# For example, llms.txt is relatively new. Likewise, an old Apple ipad looks for a "precomposed"
# favicon. I would worry about what else I'm not aware of. I think it's better to let everything
# not-whitelisted fall through. But, I have considered blocking "/js" if there was a lot of probing
# that.
#
# RULE #2: Block: requests for things I don't have (and look malicious):
#
# So far I am blocking:

# I get hundreds of requests for /aa, /ab, /ac, etc. The largest path I serve is /ads.txt (8 char).
# There shouldn't be any requests for any files with paths shorter than that. I could probably
# block < 10, but there's alawys new standards evolving (like /llms.txt which is 9). I wouldn't want
# to block something that's normal someday (unforeseen today). I think there's almost no chance of a
# future standard looking for something shorter than 8.
len(http.request.uri.path) lt 8 or

# I block paths ending in these strings:
substring(lower(http.request.uri.path), -3) in {".gz" ".7z" ".xz" ".md" ".sh" ".js" ".rc" ".db" ".py" "yml"} or
substring(lower(http.request.uri.path), -4) in {".zip" ".tar" ".rar" ".bz2" ".tgz" ".cfm" ".ini" ".asp" ".cfg" ".map" ".sql" ".axd" ".cgi" ".bak" ".log" ".key" ".pem" "dump" ".hcl" "vars"} or
substring(lower(http.request.uri.path), -5) in {".json" ".toml" ".aspx" ".yaml" ".conf" "state" ".lock" "phtml"} or

# I block paths starting with these strings:
substring(http.request.uri.path, 0, 2) in {"/_" "/~" "/$" "/;"} or
substring(lower(http.request.uri.path), 0, 3) in {"/v2" "/s3" "/.;"} or
substring(lower(http.request.uri.path), 0, 4) in {"/.ds" "/.ht" "/aws" "/ecp" "/var" "/log" "/opt" "/etc" "/rsc" "/old" "/pre" "/dev" "/new" "/asp" "/..;" "/id_" "/img" "/v1/" "/.ai"} or
substring(lower(http.request.uri.path), 0, 5) in {"/.ssh" "/.aws" "/alfa" "/blog" "/.svn" "/root" "/rest" "/team" "/home" "/file" "/page" "/post" "/.bod" "/auto" "/cred" "/user" "/word" "/inst" "/curl" "/host" "/feed" "/sign" "/test" "/dash" "/boot" "/info" "/.pgp" "/make" "/mini"} or
substring(lower(http.request.uri.path), 0, 6) in {"/graph" "/debug" "/login" "/admin" "/oauth" "/media" "/react" "/theme" "/tinyf" "/.idea" "/.bash" "/check" "/front" "/alvin" "/nginx" "/.wind" "/.curs"} or
substring(lower(http.request.uri.path), 0, 7) in {"/upload" "/plugin" "/.trash" "/server" "/health" "/action" "/webjar" "/orders" "/editor" "/assets" "/module" "/static" "/manage" "/update" "/vendor"} or
substring(lower(http.request.uri.path), 0, 8) in {"/.pypirc" "/storage" "/request" "/cgi-bin" "/swagger" "/console" "/webhook" "/setting" "/project" "/product" "/package" "/message" "/control" "/contact" "/billing" "/.vercel" "/.stripe" "/jenkins" "/profile" "/payment" "/threads" "/enhance" "/.yarnrc" "/metrics" "/web-inf" "/.action" "/staging" "/laravel" "/deliver" "/details" "/pricing"} or
substring(lower(http.request.uri.path), 0, 9) in {"/procfile" "/actuator" "/telescop" "/firebase" "/filezill" "/.netlify" "/articles" "/register" "/template" "/componen" "/.amplify" "/livewire" "/official" "/.profile"} or

# Each Cloudflare rule is limited to 4096 chars (and you get 5 rules). Doing it as above uses less
# chars than individual "contains" or "ends_with" functions. Especially if you have to use the lower
# function on them too. It adds up. 
#
# These are things that can occur anywhere in the path. You may or may not need "lower" to catch 
# mixed-case uses.
http.request.uri.path contains "wp" or
lower(http.request.uri.path) contains "php" or
http.request.uri.path contains "env" or
http.request.uri.path contains "[" or
http.request.uri.path contains "application.properties" or
http.request.uri.path contains "/wordpress" or
http.request.uri.path contains "/auth" or
http.request.uri.path contains "/.git" or
http.request.uri.path contains "config" or
http.request.uri.path contains "backup" or
http.request.uri.path contains "api" or
http.request.uri.path contains "debug" or
lower(http.request.uri.path) contains "docker" or
http.request.uri.path contains "%" or
http.request.uri.path contains "*" or
http.request.uri.path contains "META-INF" or
http.request.uri.path contains "/login" or
http.request.uri.path contains "credential" or
http.request.uri.path contains "gradle"

# FWIW: "wp," "php," ".git" & "env" block 90% of the malicious requests. Adding more rules is 
# diminishing returns.

#
# ORDER OF PROCESSING
#
# Remember: security "custom rules" are processed before "bulk redirects." That's good because it
# wouldn't make sense to redirect hundreds (thousands) of requests only to be blocked. (In the prior
# section about bulk-redirects, I mentioned another place redirects can be done. Those are processed
# before these security rules.).
#
# [1] https://developers.cloudflare.com/ruleset-engine/reference/phases-list/#request-phases
#
# [2] https://developers.cloudflare.com/rules/url-forwarding/bulk-redirects/how-it-works/#url-matching-algorithm
